        comment     2026-11-19T01:43:17Z
            Ref by definition (finding; decision 1 is the user's): not needed at run time, needed by the types. Two
            machines that send to each other import each other's Machine; ES modules evaluate the cycle fine because
            `send` reads its callbacks only in a turn (a scratch run of real `machine()` across a cycle loaded).
            TypeScript does not: without an annotation both report TS7022 (implicitly any, referenced in its own
            initializer), and so does any machine that sends to its own kind (`Household`, `Git/Mirror`). One `const X:
            Machine<typeof Def>` breaks it, and a ref built from the definition (a scratch `refOf(Def, input)`)
            needs none.

            Decisions for the user (proposed, each with my recommendation):
            1. Cyclic sends: A, document the `Machine<typeof Def>` annotation (the fixtures use it); B, `actorRef` takes
               a definition. Recommend A: no new concept, standard TypeScript.
            2. A machine with no input is named only by a literal (`RefName`), so a send to a name held in memory does
               not type-check (it hid the transfers cell). A, relax `RefName` to `string`, the run-time check refusing a
               `~` name on the sender (R14); B, keep it. Recommend A.
            3. A send cannot reach the actor its own context is wired to, so fixtures name targets by literal
               ("checkout-1", "client"). A, leave it (a rare escape hatch); B, a later proposal. Recommend A, logged in
               revisit.md if accepted.
            4. The booting row has no Story: no kept app has a persisted sender owed an ending at boot. A, leave it to
               @task.V3.T9's boot queue; B, give `CheckoutFlow` an operation. Recommend A.
            5. A self-continuation (`Household`'s `Next`) must be a public event, since `send` sends only public events.
               Recommend leaving it: a host sending `Next` only starts the next room.

            Small rulings (mine): `send`'s callbacks read `{ memory, event }` (`SendArg`), the after-memory as actions
            do; Actors R20's "stops a send's restore only once the removal is acknowledged" loses to R16's NoRecord for
            an actor being disposed (the Spec edit comes with the build, specs.log note).
