        comment     2026-11-19T04:36:34Z
            Design (proposed, the lead): one admission table and one exit channel, run as one ordered disposal; no guard
            per entry point.

            - (A) A guard per call: runPromise checks the life, dispose catches Scope.close's failure, the fiber
              observer checks interrupt cleanups, forget checks the operation. Four places kept in step by hand; a call
              fiber's Exit already reaches the step after Disposed and `admit` drops it, so a guard there races
              the life.
            - (B, chosen) runPromise is a row of `admit`, so I42's totality test covers it with no new guard. Every
              fiber Flow forks (calls, reads, timers, a runPromise Effect) lives in the Runtime's FiberMap, and one host
              function judges its Exit before admission, so an interrupt cleanup's failure is never dropped with a late
              result. dispose is one Effect the driver runs in order (DisposeRuntime, wait for Disposed, close the
              Scope, fold every cleanup Cause in phase order) into one Deferred, so every caller shares one result,
              CleanupFailed included.
            - (C) A child Scope per incarnation, closed consumers first: an idiomatic Scope tree, but actors own no
              finalizers (only calls and the services Layer do), so it buys an order no one can observe, mirrors the
              World in the host and allocates per actor under L1.

            Why B: correctness first, it removes the lost-cleanup and per-call-guard classes; one table and one channel
            read at a glance; its cost is one Exit inspection per settled fiber, which `resultOf` already pays. Reverse:
            per-call guards.
